<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander Pushkin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Pushkin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander Pushkin was a Russian poet, playwright and novelist of the early nineteenth century, widely regarded as the founder of modern Russian literature. His works include Eugene Onegin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boris-godunov">Boris Godunov</a>. He is cited as part of the cultural legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-pushkin.jpg" alt="Alexander Pushkin" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orest Kiprensky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orest_Kiprensky_-_%D0%9F%D0%BE%D1%80%D1%82%D1%80%D0%B5%D1%82_%D0%BF%D0%BE%D1%8D%D1%82%D0%B0_%D0%90.%D0%A1.%D0%9F%D1%83%D1%88%D0%BA%D0%B8%D0%BD%D0%B0_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally, the Khan’s Palace at Bakhchysarai, with its Fountain of Tears, inspired <b>Alexander Pushkin</b>’s poem The Fountain of Bakhchisaray (1824), and khans such as Gazi II Giray were noted poets.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian cultural influence spread through the Orthodox Church, the Russian language imposed on subject peoples from Poles to Kazakhs, and the literature and music of the nineteenth century, represented by <b>Pushkin</b>, Tolstoy, Dostoevsky, Tchaikovsky and Mussorgsky.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alexander Pushkin</b> addressed poems to Decembrist friends, Nikolai Nekrasov wrote a long poem about their wives, and Leo Tolstoy began a novel about them that grew into War and Peace.</blockquote><a href="/movement/decembrist-revolt">Decembrist revolt · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Elsewhere <b>Alexander Pushkin</b> in Russia, Adam Mickiewicz in Lithuania and later in exile, Alessandro Manzoni in Milan with his novel The Betrothed, and Ralph Waldo Emerson and the Transcendentalists in New England gave the movement national forms.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as Henry Fielding and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are Jorge Luis Borges, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, Victor Hugo, Marcel Proust, <b>Alexander Pushkin</b>, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and W. B. Yeats.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1828 Aleksandr <b>Pushkin</b> published a partial Russian version of &quot;The Twa Corbies&quot;, made from a French translation of Scott's ballads; his &quot;Scottish Song&quot;, which ends on the lady waiting for a lover who is alive, became very well known among Russian speakers and was set by many…</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexander Pushkin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Habit was given us in distress By Heaven in lieu of happiness.&#8221;</p><footer>Eugene Onegin (1833), canto 2 &#183; tr. Henry Spalding, 1881 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23997/pg23997-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Your heart possesses, I know well, Honour and pride inflexible. I love you—to what end deceive?— But I am now another’s bride— For ever faithful will abide.&#8221;</p><footer>Eugene Onegin (1833), canto 8 &#183; tr. Henry Spalding, 1881 &#183; spoken by Tatiana &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/23997/pg23997-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Heaven grant that we may never see such, a senseless and merciless revolt again!&#8221;</p><footer>The Captain's Daughter (1836) &#183; tr. T. Keane, 1894 &#183; spoken by the narrator, Pyotr Grinyov &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/55219/pg55219-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
